# Printed headings are selected explicitly instead of guessing from nearby text.
refs={1:['1.1 ABOUT MACROECONOMICS','1.2 HOW MACROECONOMICS DIFFERS FROM MICROECONOMICS?','Micro-Macro Paradox · Micro-Macro Interdependence','1.3 SCOPE AND SIGNIFICANCE OF MACROECONOMICS'],2:['2.1 MEANING OF END-USE CLASSIFICATION OF GOODS','2.1.1 Final Goods · Consumption Goods · Capital Goods','2.2 CONSUMPTION EXPENDITURE · 2.3 INVESTMENT','2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and the Concept of Depreciation','2.4 STOCKS AND FLOWS','2.5 FOUR SECTORS · 2.6 INTERSECTORAL FLOWS · 2.7 CIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME'],3:['3.1 CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME','3.1.3 Normal Residents · 3.2.2 Domestic Territory','3.2 DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL CONCEPTS OF INCOME','3.3 GROSS AND NET · 3.4 MARKET PRICE AND FACTOR COST','3.5 AGGREGATES RELATED TO NATIONAL INCOME','3.6 NOMINAL AND REAL GDP','3.7 GDP AND WELFARE'],4:['4.2 VALUE ADDED METHOD (OR PRODUCT METHOD)','4.2.2 Precautions · 4.2.3 Problem of Double Counting','4.3 INCOME METHOD','4.3.2 Precautions Regarding Income Method','4.4 EXPENDITURE METHOD','4.5 RECONCILIATION OF THREE METHODS · 4.6 TREATMENT OF DIFFERENT ITEMS'],5:['5.1 MEANING AND EVOLUTION OF MONEY','5.2 FORMS OF MONEY','5.3 FUNCTIONS OF MONEY','5.4 SUPPLY OF MONEY','ABILITY ZONE · Measures of Money Supply · Liquidity'],6:['6.1 COMMERCIAL BANKS','6.2 MONEY CREATION BY THE COMMERCIAL BANKS','6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK','6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control','6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments · Qualitative Instruments','ABILITY ZONE · Success of Repo Rate · Success of Open Market Operations'],7:['7.1 CONCEPT OF AD · 7.2 COMPONENTS OF AD · 7.3 CONCEPT OF AS','7.4 CONSUMPTION FUNCTION','APC vs. MPC','SAVING FUNCTION · Break-even Point','APS vs. MPS · Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save','INVESTMENT · ABILITY ZONE: Derivation of C-function from S-function'],8:['8.1 CONCEPT OF SHORT RUN · 8.2 CONCEPT OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT','Equilibrium GDP · Inventory Adjustment','Ex-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment','Effective Demand · Underemployment Equilibrium','8.5 INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER','Multiplier Mechanism'],9:['9.1 SOME CRITICAL CONCEPTS','DEFICIENT DEMAND · Deflationary Gap','EXCESS DEMAND · Inflationary Gap','Fiscal Policy','Monetary Policy'],10:['10.1 CONCEPT OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET · 10.2 OBJECTIVES','10.3.1 Budget Receipts','Tax Revenue · Non-tax Revenue','10.3.2 Budget Expenditure','10.4 BUDGET DEFICIT: REVENUE DEFICIT, FISCAL DEFICIT AND PRIMARY DEFICIT','10.5 BALANCED AND UNBALANCED BUDGET','ABILITY ZONE · Public Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods'],11:['11.1 FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE RATE','11.2.1 Flexible Exchange Rate','Appreciation and Depreciation of Domestic Currency','11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate','11.3 MANAGED FLOATING · 11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS','11.7 FOREIGN EXCHANGE MARKET'],12:['12.1 BoP (or BoP ACCOUNTS)','12.2.1 Current Account','12.2.2 Capital Account','12.2.3 Official Reserves Account','12.3.1 Autonomous and Accommodating Items of BoP Account','ABILITY ZONE · Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense','12.3.2 Significance of BoP Accounts · Causes of Disequilibrium']}
for c in cs:
 for i,l in enumerate(c['lessons']):l['textbookHeading']=refs[int(c['id'])][i]
def add(n,title,heading,body,source,kind='Exam points',formula=''):
 c=cs[n-1];c['lessons'].append(dict(title=title,textbookHeading=heading,body=body,source=source,kind=kind,formula=formula))
add(2,'Types of consumption goods','2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods','Durable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators. Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes. Non-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food. Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment. Durability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.','IMG_0504')
add(2,'Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not','ABILITY ZONE · Significance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods','Intermediate inputs are embodied in final output. Counting both adds the same value more than once. If cloth worth ₹5,000 becomes shirts worth ₹7,000, final output is ₹7,000, not ₹12,000. Under the value-added method, the cloth producer’s value and the shirt producer’s additional ₹2,000 are each counted once. It is incorrect to say intermediate goods contribute nothing: their contribution is included in final value.','IMG_0513','Ability Zone')
add(3,'Special cases of residents and territory','ABILITY ZONE · Normal Residents of a Country · Components of Domestic Territory','Normal Residents: citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic Territory: include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.','IMG_0526','Ability Zone')
add(4,'Steps under the three measurement methods','4.2.1 Steps · 4.3.1 Steps · 4.4.1 Steps','Value Added Method: identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income. Income Method: identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA. Expenditure Method: identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC. Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.','IMG_0538')
add(5,'Why money solves barter’s difficulties','5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange · 5.3 Functions of Money','Lack of Double Coincidence of Wants: money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another. Lack of a Common Measure of Value: prices expressed in money make values comparable. Difficulty in Storing Wealth: money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it. Difficulty in Deferred Payments: future payments can be fixed in monetary units. Difficulty in Transfer of Value: purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods. In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.','IMG_0546')
add(6,'Issuer of currency and banker to government','6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions','Issuer of Currency: the central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control. Banker to Government: the RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role. Distinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.','IMG_0555')
add(6,'Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house','6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions','Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role: commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning. Lender of Last Resort: the central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable. Clearing House: it settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers. Custodian of Foreign Exchange: it manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market. Control of Credit: it regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.','IMG_0556')
add(7,'Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS','Comparative Table of APC, MPC, APS and MPS · Critical Questions','APC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive. APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one. MPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved. Do not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.','IMG_0571')
add(8,'Break-even income versus equilibrium income','Consumption Function · Equilibrium Output','Break-even Income: consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y. Equilibrium Income: planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I. With positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model. If you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.','IMG_0578',formula='Break-even Y = C̄/MPS\nEquilibrium Y = (C̄ + I)/MPS')
add(10,'Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce','10.2 OBJECTIVES OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET','GDP Growth: create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives. Allocation of Resources: influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision. Provision of Public Goods: provide collective services that markets may undersupply. Redistribution of Income and Wealth: use progressive taxation and welfare spending. Balanced Regional Growth: direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions. Employment Opportunities: expand activity and labour demand. Economic Stability: raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand. Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.','IMG_0608')
add(10,'Free-rider problem','ABILITY ZONE · Free Rider Problem','A free rider enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing the full or partial cost of providing them. Non-excludability makes it difficult to withhold benefits from non-payers. Private providers may be unable to collect enough revenue to cover costs, so the market can fail to provide a needed public good. National defence and street lighting illustrate why public provision can be necessary.','IMG_0608','Ability Zone')
add(10,'Tax rates and tax burden are different','ABILITY ZONE · Progressive and Regressive Nature of Taxation is Different from Progressive and Regressive Rates of Taxation','Progressive Rate: the tax rate rises with income. Regressive Rate: the tax rate falls with income. Proportionate Rate: the tax rate stays constant as income changes. Progressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the rich. Regressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the poor. The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden. When answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.','IMG_0608','Ability Zone')
add(10,'Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged?','ABILITY ZONE · Does Balanced Budget Leave Aggregate Demand Unaffected in the Economy?','No. Equal tax collection and government expenditure do not necessarily have equal first-round effects on AD. Government expenditure enters AD in full; a tax increase reduces disposable income, and consumption falls by MPC times the tax rise. If G and taxes each rise by ₹100 and MPC is 0.5, expenditure raises AD by ₹100 while the initial consumption loss is ₹50, giving a net initial rise of ₹50. Under the simple lump-sum-tax model, later rounds imply a balanced-budget multiplier of one. Distinguish the initial AD change from the final income change.','IMG_0608','Ability Zone',formula='Initial ΔAD = ΔG − MPC × ΔT\nIf ΔG = ΔT: initial ΔAD = (1 − MPC)ΔG')
add(11,'Fixed versus flexible rates: named merits and demerits','11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS OF FIXED AND FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE','Fixed Rate—Merits: market stability and mobility of capital; growth of international trade; stable macroeconomic policies; devaluation as a policy tool; a check on inflation. Fixed Rate—Demerits: large reserve requirements; inefficient allocation of resources; limited forex market; discouraged venture capital; possible divergence from equilibrium. Flexible Rate—Merits: fewer reserves required for rate defence; market allocation of resources; larger forex market; international mobility of liquidity; support for venture capital. Flexible Rate—Demerits: weak-currency marginalisation; market uncertainty; difficulty maintaining stable monetary policy; difficulty with bilateral trade agreements; external shocks. These are the textbook’s headings: explain the relevant mechanism instead of only listing them.','IMG_0616')
